---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import { courses } from "../data/courses";
import { u } from "../lib/u";

const py = courses.filter((c) => c.categorySlug === "python" || /python/i.test(c.title));
const site = "https://coursedepth.com";
const url = `${site}/udemy-python-course-reviews/`;

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Which Python course has the best reviews?",
    a: "Dr. Angela Yu's \"100 Days of Code: The Complete Python Pro Bootcamp\" has the highest rating of the top Python courses we track — 4.7 out of 5 from 437,756 students. Jose Portilla's \"Complete Python Bootcamp\" has more reviews overall (566,376) at 4.6 out of 5. Both are strong picks: choose 100 Days of Code if you learn best by building small projects daily, and the Complete Python Bootcamp if you want one long, reference-style course.",
  },
  {
    q: "Are Python courses good for complete beginners?",
    a: "Yes. Both top Python courses are rated for beginners — they start from variables and loops and progress to projects, libraries and web basics. Check that the last update date is within the last 12 months so examples match the current Python version.",
  },
  {
    q: "How long do Python courses take to finish?",
    a: "The two biggest beginner bootcamps run about 30–60 hours of video. At a steady pace of one hour a day, that is roughly one to two months. Project-based courses take longer because you build alongside the lessons, but the practice is what makes the material stick.",
  },
  {
    q: "Do Python courses include a certificate?",
    a: "Most top-rated Python courses include a certificate of completion and a 30-day money-back guarantee. The certificate proves attendance, not competence — see our guide on whether course certificates are worth it.",
  },
];

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: site + "/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Python Course Reviews", item: url },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: "Python Course Reviews 2026 — Real Student Ratings",
      description: "Honest Python course reviews: real student ratings, review counts, update dates and which top Python bootcamp fits you.",
      datePublished: "2026-10-21",
      dateModified: "2026-10-21",
      inLanguage: "en",
      author: { "@type": "Organization", name: "CourseDepth", url: site + "/" },
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "CourseDepth", url: site + "/", logo: { "@type": "ImageObject", url: site + "/images/logo-mark.svg" } },
      mainEntityOfPage: { "@type": "WebPage", "@id": url },
      speakable: { "@type": "SpeakableSpecification", cssSelector: [".lead"] },
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({ "@type": "Question", name: f.q, acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: f.a } })),
    },
  ],
};
---
<Layout type="article" title="Python Course Reviews 2026 — Real Student Ratings | CourseDepth" description="Honest Python course reviews: real student ratings, review counts, update dates and which top Python bootcamp fits you.">
<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(jsonLd)} />
<Header />

<section class="page-hero"><div class="container">
  <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href={u("/")}>Home</a> <span aria-hidden="true">›</span> <span>Python course reviews</span></nav>
  <h1>Python Course Reviews</h1>
  <p>Real student ratings and update history for the best Python courses in 2026.</p>
</div></section>

<section class="section"><div class="container" style="max-width:760px">
  <div class="article-meta">
    <b>Updated 21 October 2026</b>
    <span>· Reviewed by the CourseDepth editorial team · Data from {py.length} tracked Python courses</span>
    <span>· 5 min read</span>
  </div>

  <div class="article-intro">
    <p><strong>Short answer:</strong> The best-reviewed Python courses are Angela Yu's <b>100 Days of Code</b> (4.7★ from 437,756 students) and Jose Portilla's <b>Complete Python Bootcamp</b> (4.6★ from 566,376 students). Both were updated in 2026 and include a certificate and a 30-day money-back guarantee.</p>
    <p>This page compares the top Python courses by rating, reviews, level and update date, plus what students actually say in their reviews.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="takeaways">
    <b>Key takeaways</b>
    <ul>
      <li><b>100 Days of Code</b> wins on rating (4.7★) — best for beginners who learn by building a project every day.</li>
      <li><b>Complete Python Bootcamp</b> has the most reviews (566K) — the broadest reference-style curriculum.</li>
      <li>Both courses were <b>updated in 2026</b> and include a certificate plus a 30-day money-back guarantee.</li>
      <li>Star rating alone is not enough — always check the <b>last update date</b> and recent reviews.</li>
      <li>Look for courses with projects and exercises, not just hours of video.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>Top Python courses compared</h2>
  <p>Current ratings, review counts and update dates for the Python courses we track, checked on 21 October 2026:</p>
  <table class="cmp">
    <thead><tr><th>Course</th><th>Rating</th><th>Reviews</th><th>Level</th><th>Updated</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {py.map((c) => (
        <tr>
          <td><a href={u(`/course/${c.slug}`)}>{c.title}</a></td>
          <td>{c.rating}★</td>
          <td>{c.reviews.toLocaleString("en-US")}</td>
          <td>{c.level}</td>
          <td>{c.updated}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>What students actually say in Python course reviews</h2>
  <p>Across the two biggest Python bootcamps, positive reviews repeat the same three themes: <b>project-based teaching</b> (students write code from the first hour), <b>instructor clarity</b> (Jose Portilla and Angela Yu both average high instructor sub-scores), and <b>breadth</b> (automation, files, OOP, libraries, web basics).</p>
  <p>The most common criticism is <b>length</b>. Long bootcamps take 30–60 hours to complete, and some sections age faster than others — reviews that praise the core but flag "some videos look dated" are usually pointing at third-party libraries that have released major versions. That is exactly why update recency matters more than the headline star rating.</p>

  <h2>How to judge Python course reviews before you buy</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Update date first.</b> A 4.9★ course last updated in 2022 will teach deprecated syntax and broken links.</li>
    <li><b>Read the 3★ reviews</b> — they are the most specific about gaps in the curriculum.</li>
    <li><b>Sort reviews by recency</b> and look for comments from the last six months.</li>
    <li><b>Check the preview videos.</b> Ten minutes of lecture tells you more than 100,000 reviews about teaching style.</li>
    <li><b>Look at the curriculum list,</b> not just the promises in the description.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What to check before starting a Python course</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Python version.</b> The course should cover a current version and mention the version it targets.</li>
    <li><b>Projects and exercises.</b> A course that only shows code is easy to watch and hard to learn from — look for builds you can repeat.</li>
    <li><b>Prerequisites.</b> Beginner courses should start from installation, variables and loops. Advanced courses should say what you already need to know.</li>
    <li><b>Update recency.</b> Libraries and tooling change quickly; prefer a course updated within the last 12 months.</li>
    <li><b>Refund policy.</b> A 30-day money-back guarantee gives you a safety net if the teaching style does not fit.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For how we weigh ratings and freshness, see <a href={u("/how-we-rate-courses")}>how we rate courses</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="faq">Python course reviews — common questions</h2>
  {faqs.map((f) => (
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>{f.q}</h3>
      <p class="faq-a">{f.a}</p>
    </div>
  ))}

  <div class="related">
    <b>Related guides</b>
    <a href={u("/programming/python")}>All Python courses</a> ·
    <a href={u("/are-udemy-courses-worth-it")}>Are online courses worth it?</a> ·
    <a href={u("/are-udemy-reviews-fake")}>Are course reviews fake?</a> ·
    <a href={u("/how-to-choose-an-online-course")}>How to choose an online course</a>
  </div>
</div></section>

<Footer />
</Layout>
